External: MUFG_T24.dll (shared base: host base class, RepositorioMaestro, Parametrizacion, ControlServicio, Alertas, XmlHelper, Config) via D:\ServiceCMS\; Apache.NMS / Apache.NMS.ActiveMQ 2.2.0 via D:\ServiceCMS\; SimpleInjector 5.0.0 (NuGet); MSTest 2.2.10 + Moq 4.20.72 (NuGet). Database: TOMI (tbl_fcm_intercambio and the operation tables). Broker: ActiveMQ (eight response queues + one input queue, names read from system parameters).
What this solution actually does: a timer-driven Windows service (interval from the encrypted service configuration, default 10 s fallback at ServiceHost.cs:33; the shared host base serialises ticks with a lock). On each tick, inside a per-tick DI scope, it (1) updates the service's execution marker, (2) reads the system date, business-day flag and the FCM_MQ_* parameters, (3) skips the tick on a non-business day or outside FCM_MQ_HORA_INICIO..FCM_MQ_HORA_FIN (raising an alert row), (4) opens an ActiveMQ connection, (5) reads every tbl_fcm_intercambio row in status INI via usp_ObtenerOperacionFCM, publishes each stored message (msg_in) to the FCM input queue and sets the row to SENT, then (6) drains eight response queues (OK, HIT, default, error, and four notification queues: approve, cancel, default, error). For each response it extracts folio and tipo_operacion from the XML payload, maps the queue to a plan status (PROC/RECV/DEF/ERR/RECC), updates the business table's nf_estatus through usp_ActualizaEstatusDinamico (table name from a whitelist), applies the SPID cut-off cancellation rule, and records the outcome (estatus, estatus_FCM, msg_out) in tbl_fcm_intercambio through usp_ActualizaEstatusNotINIFCM. At the end of the tick it closes the shared MQ connection and writes the termination marker.
