5. Architecture-Fit Note
FCMConector is a timer-driven poll/bridge: DB-poll -> publish to one queue -> drain eight queues -> DB-update, once per tick (the shared host guarantees non-overlapping ticks) inside a business-day window; it is neither a continuous listener nor a multi-step saga, although the INI -> SENT -> PROC/RECV/ERR/RECC lifecycle is saga-like. A Windows service is a reasonable hosting model for it today and nothing in the findings argues for a re-platform. Following the sequencing in `_ORCHESTRATION.md` §6 (./_ORCHESTRATION.md): first fix the defects (FCM-FR-1, 2, 3, 4, then 5 and 6), then add tests and remove duplication (FCM-FR-8, 15, 16), and only then evaluate whether polling should become a broker-driven MessageListener with a transactional session (which would make FR-4/5 disappear structurally) or an outbox publisher. Unknown and needed for that decision: the real tick interval and window, volume per day, the FCM engine's latency/de-duplication contract, ActiveMQ redelivery/DLQ configuration, whether the broker allows many connections, and hosting constraints.
